  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 233 AM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nDrier air in SW flow aloft will remain in place across the area \ntoday.  However, there is a decent upper level jet over the area \nwhich will combine with decent lapse rates and lingering moisture, \nto produce a chance of showers/tstms over the higher terrain, mainly \nnorth of I-70.  With decent mid level flow, gusty winds will \naccompany this activity as well.  Meanwhile, along northern areas of \nthe I-25 Corridor, will see sct high based showers along with a \npossible tstm by late aftn. DCAPE is around 1000 j/kg so brief \ngusty outflow winds will occur with this activity as well. For \ntonight, the upper level jet will move slowly across the plains \nwhich may lead to an additional chc of higher based showers and a \nfew tstms thru the evening hours. As for highs today, readings \nwill rise into the upper 80's to lower 90s over the plains.\n\nOn Thu, SW flow aloft will continue but it appears an even drier \nairmass will be in place. Thus don't expect any shower/tstm \nactivity. Temps will continue to warm with readings rising into \nthe lower to mid 90's across nern CO.\n\nBy Fri, the flow aloft will become more SSW.  Some monsoon moisture \ncould get entrained into the flow.  At this point, still think any \nshower/tstm activty in the aftn/evening will be widely sct. Once \nagain highs will reach the lower to mid 90's over nern CO.\n\nFor the weekend, models still show an upper level trough over the \nwrn US with an upper level high extending from the srn Rockies into \nthe central US.  As a result, this will leave SSW flow aloft across \nthe area as some monsoon moisture moves across the area.  It's not \ncertain how deep this moisture will be initially.  Specific Humidity \nis in the 5-6 g/kg range on Sat and then increases to 7-8 g/kg on \nSun. Thus, activity on Sat may be rather high based and more sct \nas compared to Sun which may have better coverage. Meanwhile, it \nstill appears highs will remain above normal both days with more \n90's possible over nern CO.\n\nBy Labor Day, the upper level trough over the wrn US is fcst to move \nnortheast across the nrn Rockies.  As a result, this leaves nrn CO \nin SW flow aloft.  Models continue to show monsoon moisture embedded \nin the flow with specific humidity from 6-8 g/kg.  Thus will keep in \na good chc of showers/tstms.  Highs still look to remain above \nnormal across the plains. \n\nFor Tue and Wed, there are some disagreements between the ECMWF and \nGFS.  The ECMWF has more of a zonal component developing as monsoon \nmoisture is shifted southward.  Meanwhile, the GFS keeps SW flow \naloft across the area with a continued influx of monsoon moisture. \nThus, the ECMWF would have drier conditions both days while the GFS \nwould have a chc of showers/tstms.",
